WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.460
Most countries have a central bank, the puppet&nbsp;
master of the economy, the bank of all banks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.540
for that country. The central bank, which we&nbsp;
call the Fed here in the U.S., is run by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.860
government. So their job is to set the rules or&nbsp;
policies that all the other banks have to follow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.900
And the central bank isn’t motivated by profit,&nbsp;
but rather their job is to babysit the economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.280
to keep it growing, to make sure people have jobs,&nbsp;
to make sure that prices don't fluctuate too much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.380
so that we can keep growing nicely. No slowdown,&nbsp;
no recession. That is what the Fed is there for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.520
They pull strings in the economy to get&nbsp;
us to spend our money in a certain way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:37.920
which in turn affects how much businesses raise&nbsp;
or drop their prices. And guess what? It totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:43.140
works. One of the strings that they have to pull&nbsp;
in the economy is called the interest rate. Want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.040
to borrow money to buy a car or a house or expand&nbsp;
your business? You're going to be way more likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.440
to do that if you only have to pay 2% interest on&nbsp;
that loan as opposed to like 6%. Lower interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.660
rates equal people and businesses want to borrow&nbsp;
and spend money. We're like freakin' puppets.

